  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday through Friday: The trough over the region early this\nmorning will dive south and develop into a closed low over\nnorthern California this afternoon. This combined with drier air\naloft moving in will allow for mostly clear skies for Wednesday\nalong with warmer temperatures with highs mainly in the 70s. On\nThursday begins to lift northeast, reaching southeast Oregon in\nthe afternoon. Increasing mid level moisture and instability moves\nnorth as well, reaching southeast Washington into the Central\nPanhandle Mountains with 700-500mb MU CAPE around 200-500 J/KG,\nwith higher values to the south over the Clearwaters. Inverted V\nsoundings suggests gusty outflow winds to be the main hazard, with\nsome CAM's models showing isolated gusts of 30-40 MPH on the Camas\nPrairie into the Central Panhandle Mountains. On Friday the low\ncontinues its northeast track but slows, moving into central and\nsouthern Idaho by the evening. Continue moisture and instability\nfrom the Blue Mountains into the Central Panhandle Mountains will\nresult in additional chances for showers and thunderstorms.\nAlthough the axis of best instability does shift east on Friday, \nsupporting overall a lower thunderstorm threat (5-15%) compared to\nThursday (15-20%).\n\nSaturday through Tuesday: On Saturday the low ejects away from the\nregion quickly, and replaced by an upper ridge supporting a warm\nand dry weekend with highs in the mid 70s to low 80s. On Monday \nand Tuesday the ridge begins to flatten but strong enough to \ndeflect any precipitation north of the region. A slightly cooling \ntrend is expected with the flattening ridge. JW",
